gmp's "long long reliability" probe — the one that decides whether the
compiler works at all — calls g() with six arguments after declaring it
`void g(){}`. Under C17 and earlier that is a function with an unspecified
parameter list, so the call is legal. C23 redefined `()` to mean `(void)`,
so gcc 16 (C23 by default) rejects the call:

    error: too many arguments to function 'g'

The probe then fails, gmp concludes the compiler is broken, and configure
either falls back to another compiler or gives up.

Upstream fixed this in 8e7bb4ae7a18 by giving g() the prototype the call
site actually uses. Fedora ships the same change as gmp-6.3.0-c23.patch,
Debian as fix-acinclude-m4-for-gcc-15.patch, and Arch as gmp-gcc-15.patch.

All three of them patch `acinclude.m4`, which is the right place, and then
re-run autoreconf. Peios packages no autoconf/automake/libtool, so we cannot
regenerate `configure` — and `configure` is where the probe actually runs,
the macro having been expanded into it verbatim (twice). So this applies the
same one-line change to both expansions in the shipped `configure` instead.
It is otherwise identical to upstream, and becomes unnecessary the moment
gmp releases a version containing 8e7bb4ae7a18.

This replaces an earlier workaround that pinned the whole build to
`-std=gnu17`, which fixed the probe by making the entire library compile as
C17 — a much larger hammer, and one that quietly rots as gcc moves on.

--- a/configure
+++ b/configure
@@ -6568,7 +6568,7 @@

 #if defined (__GNUC__) && ! defined (__cplusplus)
 typedef unsigned long long t1;typedef t1*t2;
-void g(){}
+void g(int a,t1 const*b,t1 c,t2 d,t1 const*e,int f){}
 void h(){}
 static __inline__ t1 e(t2 rp,t2 up,int n,t1 v0)
 {t1 c,x,r;int i;if(v0){c=1;for(i=1;i<n;i++){x=up[i];r=x+1;rp[i]=r;}}return c;}
@@ -8187,7 +8187,7 @@

 #if defined (__GNUC__) && ! defined (__cplusplus)
 typedef unsigned long long t1;typedef t1*t2;
-void g(){}
+void g(int a,t1 const*b,t1 c,t2 d,t1 const*e,int f){}
 void h(){}
 static __inline__ t1 e(t2 rp,t2 up,int n,t1 v0)
 {t1 c,x,r;int i;if(v0){c=1;for(i=1;i<n;i++){x=up[i];r=x+1;rp[i]=r;}}return c;}
